%U A400208 1551121004333098598400000,3102242008666197196800000,109759804060216989780541440000000,530505719624382117272616960000000,65782709233423382541804503040000000
%N A400208 Integers m such that m^2 = p! * q! * r! for some p > q > r > 1.
%C A400208 The Problem 1 of Round Two of the 2023 New Zealand Mathematical Olympiad asks whether there exist infinitely many triples of positive integers (p > q > r > 1) such that p! * q! * r! is a perfect square. The answer is yes; see the official solutions in link.
%C A400208 The inequalities p > q > r > 1 exclude trivial solutions such as k! * k! * 1! = (k!)^2.
%C A400208 Several infinite families can be exhibited for this Diophantine equation.
%C A400208 (1) The subsequence A000197:
%C A400208 Indeed, terms of the form (t!)! for t >= 3, satisfy the identity: ((t!)!)^2 = (t!)! * (t! - 1)! * t! with (p,q,r) = (t!, t!-1, t).
%C A400208 This is a first proof for the Olympiad problem.
%C A400208 For t = 3, we get a(1) with 720^2 = 6! * 5! * 3!.
%C A400208 For t = 4, we get a(9) with 620448401733239439360000^2 = 24! * 23! * 4!.
%C A400208 The case t = 2 gives 2^2 = 2! * 1! * 2! but it does not satisfy p > q > r > 1.
%C A400208 (2) The family (q+1, q, r):
%C A400208 If p = q+1, then p! * q! = (q+1)! * q! = (q+1)*(q!)^2. Consequently, p!q!r! is a square if and only if (q+1) * r! is a square.
%C A400208 For any fixed integer r = k > 1, we may take q+1 = k! * x^2 avec x >= 1.
%C A400208 Then (q+1) * r!  = (k! * x^2) * k! = (k! * x)^2, and therefore (p, q, r) = (k! * x^2, k! * x^2 - 1, k) gives a solution.
%C A400208 This proves the stronger statement that, for every fixed (r > 1), there are infinitely many q such that (q +1)! * q! * r! is a perfect square (second proof for the Olympiad problem).
%C A400208 For r = 2, taking x = 2, 3, 4 gives respectively:
%C A400208    a(3): 20160^2 = 8! * 7! * 2!,
%C A400208    a(6): 2134124568576000^2 = 18! * 17! * 2!,
%C A400208    a(14): 65782709233423382541804503040000000^2 = 32! * 31! * 2!.
%C A400208 For r = 3, taking x = 1, 2 gives respectively:
%C A400208    a(2): 1440^2 = 6! * 5! * 3!,
%C A400208    a(8): 310224200866619719680000^2 = 24! * 23! * 3!.
%C A400208 Also, there are multiplicative relations between some terms: if one of p, q, r, say p is such that p+1 = s^2 , then if p!*q!*r! = m^2, then (p+1)!*q!*r! = (s*m)^2.
%C A400208 Some examples :
%C A400208     for r = 3 and s = 2: a(2) = 2 * a(1), a(9) = 2 * a(8), a(11) = 2 * a(10);
%C A400208     for p = 8 and s = 3:  a(4) = 3 * a(3);
%C A400208     for p = 24 and s = 5: a(10) = 5 * a(8), a(11) = 5 * a(9);
%C A400208     for p = 35 and s = 6: a(16) = 6 * a(15).
%H A400208 New Zealand Mathematical Olympiad Committee, <a href="https://www.mathsolympiad.org.nz/competitions/nzmo/files/problems.php?round=2&amp;year=2023&amp;type=solutions">2023 NZMO Round 2, problem 1</a>.
%H A400208 <a href="/index/O#Olympiads">Index to sequences related to Olympiads and other Mathematical Competitions</a>.
%e A400208 a(1) = 720 since 720^2 = 6! * 5! * 3!
%e A400208 a(2) = 1440 since 1440^2 = 6! * 5! * 4!
%e A400208 a(3) = 20160 since 20160^2 = 8! * 7! * 2!
%e A400208 a(4) = 60480 since 60480^2 = 9! * 7! * 2!
%e A400208 a(5) = 3628800 since 3628800^2 = 10! * 7! * 6!
